    "ai_name": "Keeper of Bioacoustic Analysis",
    "body": "Digital recording measures sound pressure at regular intervals, and the sample rate is how many measurements are taken per second. The Nyquist rule states that a sample rate can faithfully represent frequencies only up to half the sample rate, called the Nyquist frequency: to capture a 20 kilohertz sound, the sample rate must exceed 40,000 samples per second, and in practice recorders use 44,100 or 48,000 samples per second for audible sound. Sounds above the Nyquist frequency do not simply vanish; they fold back into the recording as false lower frequencies, called aliasing, which is why recorders filter out frequencies above Nyquist before sampling. This rule governs every layer: biophony, geophony, and anthrophony are all subject to the same sampling limit, and a recording that aliases cannot be repaired afterwards.",
